# Caddy front end for Name Zone + managed URL redirects.
#
# Terminates TLS and reverse-proxies to the Next.js app on 127.0.0.1:3000
# (`next start`). Redirect hostnames such as x.craigd.rxd.zone are too deep for
# a single *.rxd.zone wildcard, so their certificates are issued ON DEMAND,
# gated by the app's authorize endpoint - Caddy asks before every issuance and
# only proceeds on HTTP 200, so a stranger hitting random.<zone> can never make
# Caddy mint a cert for an unconfigured host.
#
# Replace the {$...} placeholders (or set them in Caddy's environment).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IF CADDY IS NOT YOUR ONLY FRONT END, READ THIS
#
# This file assumes Caddy terminates TLS for every hostname. A deployment can
# just as reasonably put the management app behind nginx on one address and
# leave Caddy handling only the on-demand redirect hostnames on another:
#
#     rxd.zone           -> nginx  -> 127.0.0.1:3000
#     x.craigd.rxd.zone  -> Caddy  -> 127.0.0.1:3000
#
# In that split, the block_internal snippet below protects ONLY the hostnames
# Caddy serves. /api/internal/* stays wide open on everything nginx serves, and
# no amount of validating or reloading Caddy will change that - the config is
# correct, it simply never sees those requests. `curl -sI <host>/api/internal/x`
# and read the Server: header to find out who is actually answering.
#
# EVERY front end needs its own equivalent block. For nginx, inside each server
# block, above the location that proxies to the app:
#
#     location ^~ /api/internal/ {
#         return 403;
#     }
#
# The ^~ prefix match takes priority over regex locations, so nothing declared
# later can re-expose the path.
#
# Blocking the path publicly never breaks on-demand TLS: Caddy's `ask` calls
# 127.0.0.1:3000 directly, not through any site block. Co-hosted companion apps
# must likewise reach the app on its internal address rather than through a
# public hostname (see NAMEZONE_INTERNAL_URL in the Wave Creator repo).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

{
	# Ask the app before issuing any on-demand certificate. Runs on localhost;
	# if the app isn't co-located, point this at the trusted internal address
	# and set REDIRECT_TLS_AUTH_SECRET (see below).
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/internal/tls/authorize
	}
	# email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# The internal TLS-authorize + any other /api/internal/* endpoints must never
# be reachable from the public internet - only Caddy's own on_demand_tls `ask`
# calls them, and those go straight to 127.0.0.1:3000 (not through a site
# block). Blanket-block the path on every public site below. (snippet)
#
# A snippet does nothing until it is imported: defining (block_internal) and
# forgetting the `import` in a site block leaves that host unprotected, and
# Caddy starts cleanly with no warning.
(block_internal) {
	handle /api/internal/* {
		respond 403
	}
}

# --- Management app -------------------------------------------------------
# The operator's own domains (bare zones + any primary app host). Normal
# (non-on-demand) certificates. Add every hostname the management UI serves on.
{$MANAGEMENT_HOSTS:rxd.zone, avn.zone} {
	import block_internal
	encode zstd gzip
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	}
}

# --- Managed URL redirects ------------------------------------------------
# Catch-all for every other HTTPS host (the redirect subdomains). Certificates
# are issued on demand and gated by the `ask` endpoint above. The app's
# middleware routes these to the redirect service by Host header.
#
# If the authorize endpoint cannot be network-isolated from the internet, set
# REDIRECT_TLS_AUTH_SECRET in the app and send it here, e.g.:
#   reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
#       header_up Authorization "Bearer {$REDIRECT_TLS_AUTH_SECRET}"
#   }
# (and point `ask` at the same secret-protected endpoint).
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	import block_internal
	encode zstd gzip
	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	}
}
